<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Zhongzong of Tang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/656">656</a>–710</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Li Xian, Li Zhe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Zhongzong of Tang (656–710) was a son of Emperor Gaozong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zetian</a>. He succeeded his father at the end of 683 but was deposed by his mother early in 684 and sent into exile. Restored by the coup of 705 that ended Wu Zetian's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>, he was dominated by his wife Empress Wei and died suddenly in 710, reputedly poisoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-zhongzong-of-tang.jpg" alt="Emperor Zhongzong of Tang" width="200" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Because the artists have died over 100 years, so unknown. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_Zhongzong.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Xian was born in 656 as the seventh son of Emperor Gaozong and the third son of Empress Wu, later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zetian</a>. In 657 he was created Prince of Zhou. His name was later changed to Li Zhe. In 681, after Gaozong fell ill from pills made by alchemists, he briefly exercised imperial powers as crown prince, assisted by chancellors aligned with Empress Wu. Gaozong fell ill again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 683, summoned Li Zhe and transferred imperial powers to him, probably on Empress Wu's advice, and soon died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Li Zhe took the throne as Emperor Zhongzong, but real power stayed with his mother, now empress dowager and regent. His reign lasted less than two months before she deposed him in favour of his younger brother, Emperor Ruizong. He was demoted to a princely rank and exiled to Fang Prefecture and Jun Prefecture in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>, under house arrest. On the way Princess Wei gave birth to a daughter, whom he wrapped in his own shirt, and she was named Li Guo'er, the child who was wrapped. He lived in fear that each messenger from the capital brought an order for his death, since Empress Dowager Wu had forced his brother Li Xian to suicide in 684.</p><p>In 697 the Khitan leader Sun Wanrong asked in a declaration why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zetian</a> kept Li Zhe in exile. Chancellors including Di Renjie urged her to recall him, and in 698 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. Li Dan offered to yield the position of crown prince, and Li Zhe was made crown prince on 24 October 698. On 20 February 705 the Shenlong Coup deposed Wu Zetian, and Zhongzong was restored three days later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his second reign most power lay with Empress Wei, her daughter Li Guo'er, who became Princess Anle, and Wu Sansi, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zetian</a>. Empress Wei accused his eldest surviving son, Li Chongfu, of complicity in a death, and he was sent out of the capital under heavy guard. Zhongzong had repeatedly refused Li Guo'er's wish to be made crown princess.</p><p>He died on 3 July 710. According to traditional historians, Empress Wei and her partisans put poison in a cake because she wanted to rule as an empress regnant like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zetian</a>. She installed his son Li Chongmao, aged sixteen, as Emperor Shang. Two weeks later Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> and her nephew Li Longji, the future Emperor Xuanzong, overthrew Empress Wei and the young emperor, and Ruizong was restored.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Zhongzong of Tang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his death in 683 she deposed her son <b>Zhongzong</b>, kept Ruizong as a figurehead, crushed a Tang princes' revolt and in 690 proclaimed her own Zhou dynasty, with Luoyang, renamed Shendu, as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/wu-zhou">Wu Zhou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=691" aria-label="Chang'an on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7319 921 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Zhongzong of Tang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7769 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.31,108.86&amp;year=691">What was here in 691 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
